#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133565 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "da23f1c0-678a-51c0-a2db-e12d756f9120" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating the Line Integral of a Scalar Function Using a Substitution"}] :question/id #uuid "132af6fa-454c-596c-a057-dfe531792102" :question/math-academy-id "e-6283" :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral of $f(x,y,z) = \\dfrac{x}{yz}$ along the curve parametrized by $\\mathbf{r}(t) = t^2\\, \\mathbf{i} + t \\,\\mathbf{j} + 1 \\,\\mathbf{k}$ for $t \\in [0,\\sqrt 2]$." :question/worked-solution "To calculate the line integral, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}s = \\int\\limits_a^b f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\, \\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\| \\, \\textrm{d}t\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) =t^2\\, \\mathbf{i} + t \\,\\mathbf{j} + 1 \\,\\mathbf{k}\n$$\n\nthen along the curve we have $x = t^2$, $y = t$, and $z=1$.\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$ and $\\| \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\|$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{′}(t) &= \\frac{dx}{dt}i + \\frac{dy}{dt}j + \\frac{dz}{dt}k \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dt}(t^{2})i + \\frac{d}{dt}(t)j + \\frac{d}{dt}(1)k \\\\\n&= 2ti + 1j + 0k \\\\\n&= 2ti + j \\\\\n‖r^{′}(t)‖ &= \\sqrt{(\\frac{dx}{dt})^{2} + (\\frac{dy}{dt})^{2} + (\\frac{dz}{dt})^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(2t)^{2} + 1^{2} + 0^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{4t^{2} + 1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z) = \\dfrac{x}{yz}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(r(t)) &= \\frac{t^{2}}{t \\cdot 1} = t\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_(C)f(x, y)ds &= ∫_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}f(r(t))‖r^{′}(t)‖dt \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}t\\sqrt{4t^{2} + 1}dt\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution\n\n$$\nu= 4t^2+1\n$$\n\n$$\n\\textrm d u=8t\\,\\textrm d t\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}t\\sqrt{4t^{2} + 1}dt &= ∫_{1}^{9}\\frac{1}{8}u^{1/2}du \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{8} \\cdot \\frac{2}{3}u^{3/2} \\mid _{1}^{9} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{12}[9^{3/2} - 1^{3/2}] \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{12}[27 - 1] \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{12} \\cdot 26 \\\\\n&= \\frac{13}{6}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133567 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "28f7479a-a84c-59fa-a7ea-9377c7e65b19" :knowledge-point/title "Writing the Line Integral of a Scalar Function as a Definite Integral"}] :question/id #uuid "f07dd3d3-606f-520f-a95e-04303aa32f24" :question/math-academy-id "e-6307" :question/problem "Find an integral that is equivalent to $\\displaystyle\\int\\limits_C xy^3 \\, \\textrm{d}s$, where $C$ is the curve parametrized by $\\mathbf r(t)= \\left \\langle \\cos t, \\sin t \\right\\rangle$ for $t \\in \\left(0, \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)$." :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2107/Source/Images/02b83c814a778a787ec461430e53ac92.png)\n\nThe path of integration is shown above. We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}s = \\int\\limits_a^b f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\, \\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\| \\, \\textrm{d}t\n$$\n\nHere, we have\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = xy^3\n$$\n\n. Along the path, we have $x=\\cos t$ and $y=\\sin t$, and so we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(r(t)) &= f(\\cos t, \\sin t) \\\\\n&= \\cos t \\cdot (\\sin t)^{3} \\\\\n&= \\sin^{3} t\\cos t\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$ and $\\| \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\|$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{′}(t) &= \\frac{dx}{dt}i + \\frac{dy}{dt}j \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dt}(\\cos t)i + \\frac{d}{dt}(\\sin t)j \\\\\n&=-\\sin ti + \\cos tj \\\\\n‖r^{′}(t)‖ &= \\sqrt{(\\frac{dx}{dt})^{2} + (\\frac{dy}{dt})^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(-\\sin t)^{2} + (\\cos t)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{\\sin^{2} t + \\cos^{2} t} \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_(C)xy^{3}ds &= ∫_{a}^{b}f(r(t))‖r^{′}(t)‖dt \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{π/2}\\sin^{3} t\\cos t \\cdot 1dt \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{π/2}\\sin^{3} t\\cos tdt\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C xy^3 \\, \\textrm{d}s = \\int^{\\pi/2}_{0} \\sin^3 t \\cos t \\, \\textrm{d}t\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133568 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "481a6b6e-05a2-5003-9ac7-b4876ffa4ffa" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating the Line Integral of a Scalar Function"}] :question/id #uuid "8bf6a863-9b9f-5877-84bc-3e11d71261c9" :question/math-academy-id "e-6308" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $\\displaystyle\\int\\limits_{C}xy\\,\\textrm{d}s$, where $C$ is the curve parametrized by $\\mathbf{r}(t) = 3t\\,\\mathbf{i}+4t\\,\\mathbf{j}\\,$ for $0\\leq t\\leq 1$." :question/worked-solution "To calculate the line integral, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}s = \\int\\limits_a^b f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\, \\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\| \\, \\textrm{d}t\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = 3t\\,\\mathbf{i}+4t\\,\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nthen along the curve we have $x=3t$ and $y=4t$.\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$ and $\\| \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\|$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{′}(t) &= \\frac{dx}{dt}i + \\frac{dy}{dt}j \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dt}(3t)i + \\frac{d}{dt}(4t)j \\\\\n&= 3i + 4j \\\\\n‖r^{′}(t)‖ &= \\sqrt{(\\frac{dx}{dt})^{2} + (\\frac{dy}{dt})^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{3^{2} + 4^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{5^{2}} \\\\\n&= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if\n\n$$\nf(x,y) =xy\n$$\n\nthen we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(r(t)) &= 3t \\cdot 4t \\\\\n&= 12t^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_(C)xyds &= ∫_{0}^{1}f(r(t))‖r^{′}(t)‖dt \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}12t^{2} \\cdot 5dt \\\\\n&= 60∫_{0}^{1}t^{2}dt \\\\\n&= 60[\\frac{t^{3}}{3}]_{0}^{1} \\\\\n&= 20[t^{3}]_{0}^{1} \\\\\n&= 20\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134201 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "28f7479a-a84c-59fa-a7ea-9377c7e65b19" :knowledge-point/title "Writing the Line Integral of a Scalar Function as a Definite Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aaecec07-7ce6-5496-8625-5a403ef6a617" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2∫_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}t\\sqrt{4t^{2} + 1}dt$." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131649} {:answer/id #uuid "ab354680-e0b0-5478-a1d7-f58e45ad7cfd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$∫_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}t\\sqrt{2t^{2} + 1}dt$." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131650} {:answer/id #uuid "ce8f850c-dcc8-5138-b786-f2b72b9b60c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\sqrt{2}∫_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}t\\sqrt{2t^{2} - 1}dt$." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131651} {:answer/id #uuid "24c4df28-efa9-56de-8caa-24248f8eb065" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4∫_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}t\\sqrt{8t^{2} + 1}dt$." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131652} {:answer/id #uuid "7e3ac233-5443-559c-9694-e0ea7b1d5e75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4∫_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}t\\sqrt{4t^{2} + 1}dt$." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131653}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7e3ac233-5443-559c-9694-e0ea7b1d5e75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4∫_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}t\\sqrt{4t^{2} + 1}dt$." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131653} :answer-field/id #uuid "7d7ec2d1-db05-533b-a070-70c547675260" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131648}] :question/id #uuid "13f50e74-e351-581a-98f5-c8e27aa5380d" :question/math-academy-id "q-40253" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to $∫_{C}f(x, y)ds$, where $f(x, y) = \\sqrt{2}(x - y)$ and the curve $C$ is parametrized\n\nby $r(t) = ⟨t^{2} + t, t^{2} - t⟩$ for $t ∈ [0, \\sqrt{2}]$?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134431 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "da23f1c0-678a-51c0-a2db-e12d756f9120" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating the Line Integral of a Scalar Function Using a Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8aac0a69-983b-55a2-8535-ced2b1a3b725" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133029} {:answer/id #uuid "e3fb7a1d-4d8d-537c-911a-46f68e16967a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "98" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133030} {:answer/id #uuid "1c592a0a-288d-5711-aaf0-305de5dc4487" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "56" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133031} {:answer/id #uuid "557d35ea-b1ee-571b-81c2-a58ed9993315" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133032} {:answer/id #uuid "617727b8-e640-5189-b2e6-70d6fe085f75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "75" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133033}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e3fb7a1d-4d8d-537c-911a-46f68e16967a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "98" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133030} :answer-field/id #uuid "04f8f545-9d9f-5d1d-ad01-ab6d37e3dda6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133028}] :question/id #uuid "a42a0c64-6afa-5778-a107-25576497d815" :question/math-academy-id "q-76186" :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral of the function $f(x, y) = \\frac{12x}{y - 1}$ along the curve parametrized by $r(t) = t^{2}i + (t + 1)j$ for $t ∈ [\\sqrt{2}, \\sqrt{6}]$."}]}
